{"type":"standard","title":"Graph of a function","displaytitle":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Graph of a function</span></span>","namespace":{"id":0,"text":""},"wikibase_item":"Q182598","titles":{"canonical":"Graph_of_a_function","normalized":"Graph of a function","display":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Graph of a function</span></span>"},"pageid":87352,"thumbnail":{"source":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Polynomial_of_degree_three.svg/330px-Polynomial_of_degree_three.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","width":330,"height":330},"originalimage":{"source":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Polynomial_of_degree_three.svg/960px-Polynomial_of_degree_three.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","width":900,"height":900},"lang":"en","dir":"ltr","revision":"1371539897","tid":"23179b19-a1b8-11f1-bc91-5977f713f6d6","timestamp":"2026-08-27T01:39:26Z","description":"Representation of a mathematical function","description_source":"local","content_urls":{"desktop":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graph_of_a_function","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graph_of_a_function?action=history","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graph_of_a_function?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Graph_of_a_function"},"mobile":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graph_of_a_function","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:History/Graph_of_a_function","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graph_of_a_function?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Graph_of_a_function"}},"extract":"In mathematics, the graph of a function  is the set of ordered pairs , where  In the common case where  and  are real numbers, these pairs are Cartesian coordinates of points in a plane and often form a curve.\nThe graphical representation of the graph of a function is also known as a plot.","extract_html":"<p>In mathematics, the <b>graph of a function</b> <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/132e57acb643253e7810ee9702d9581f159a1c61\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.671ex;width:1.279ex;height:2.509ex\" /></span> is the set of ordered pairs <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/41cf50e4a314ca8e2c30964baa8d26e5be7a9386\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.838ex;width:5.328ex;height:2.843ex\" /></span>, where <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/de09d5f1e42dc957864c2d2321712e2e168c1298\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.838ex;width:9.318ex;height:2.843ex\" /></span> In the common case where <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/87f9e315fd7e2ba406057a97300593c4802b53e4\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.338ex;width:1.33ex;height:1.676ex\" /></span> and <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/202945cce41ecebb6f643f31d119c514bec7a074\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.838ex;width:4.418ex;height:2.843ex\" /></span> are real numbers, these pairs are Cartesian coordinates of points in a plane and often form a curve.\nThe graphical representation of the graph of a function is also known as a <i>plot</i>.</p>"}